{
    "title": "Demurrage and Detention Billing Requirements Properly Issued Invoices Provision Set Aside by Court",
    "type": "Rule",
    "date": "December 29, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-12-29/pdf/2025-23920.pdf",
    "summary": "Two federal updates just landed: a short-term boating safety zone for a New Year\u2019s Eve fireworks show at the Lake of the Ozarks, and a court-driven change to how ports and ocean carriers can bill certain late fees.\n\nFor the Lake of the Ozarks, the Coast Guard is setting a 500\u2011foot safety zone around the launch site near mile marker 0.2 from 8:30 p.m. to 10 p.m. on December 31. The goal is straightforward\u2014keep spectators, boaters, and crews safe during the show. Local residents, visitors, and first responders benefit from the added safety and clarity. The temporary downside is limited access for boaters and water-based businesses during that 90\u2011minute window, which could mean brief delays or route changes. The agency says there\u2019s no new paperwork burden, no significant environmental impact, and no major cost to governments or the private sector.\n\nOn the shipping side, the Federal Maritime Commission is removing one part of its 2024 rule on invoices for late fees tied to containers and terminal pickups. A federal appeals court set aside the section that barred billing trucking companies in certain situations, saying the agency hadn\u2019t clearly explained how that bar fit with its own contract-based logic. In practical terms, the Commission is deleting that specific provision, while the rest of the transparency and timing requirements for these invoices remain in force. Ocean carriers and marine terminals gain clarity and a bit more flexibility about who they can bill when contracts support it. Trucking companies may now face invoices they were previously shielded from, potentially increasing their costs or the need to dispute charges. Cargo owners could see fewer billing stand\u2011offs if invoices go to the party the contract actually names, but some may lose a layer of protection if costs shift their way.\n\nBoth moves aim to reduce confusion\u2014one on the water for a holiday event, the other in the supply chain after a court ruling\u2014while creating short-term trade\u2011offs for those closest to the action.",
